    "result_text": "I implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py` and added `tests/test_claude.py`. Both tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed). The first run took 34s, but the second took about 1s and the new test itself takes 0.06s, so the delay looks like a one-time startup cost.\n\n**You can't pass the existing `httpx.Client` to Anthropic.** anthropic 1.8.0 uses `httpx2` instead of `httpx`. It raises a `TypeError` if you give it an `httpx` object (client, timeout or transport), so the client from `build_http_client()` and `app.http.TIMEOUT` can't be handed to it. Instead I build an `httpx2` client from the same pieces in `app/http.py`:\n- `EGRESS_TAG`, sent as the `X-Egress-Tag` header;\n- `log_egress`, used unchanged as the request hook;\n- `CONNECT_TIMEOUT` and `READ_TIMEOUT`, turned into an `httpx2.Timeout`.\n\n`httpx2` came with anthropic, so no new dependency was added.\n\nHow it works:\n- **Logging each retry:** the hook sits on the underlying HTTP client, and the SDK retries by sending a new request through that client. So every attempt, retries included, logs exactly one line.\n- **Timeouts:** the SDK sets a timeout on every request, which overrides the HTTP client's own timeout. So I also pass the 5s/60s timeout to `Anthropic(...)` directly; otherwise the SDK's default would be used.\n- **Transport:** I use the SDK's `DefaultHttpxClient` so its usual connection defaults stay in place. `transport` is only passed on when you give one, because passing `transport=None` would switch off the SDK's default transport setup.\n- **Stub transports:** because of the `httpx2` switch, the transport you pass must be an `httpx2` one (for example `httpx2.MockTransport`). An `httpx.MockTransport` will be rejected.\n- `max_retries=2`.\n\nThe new test makes the stub fail twice with 529 and then succeed. It checks that:\n- all three requests carry the header;\n- the timeouts are 5s connect and 60s read;\n- there are exactly three log lines on `app.egress`.\n\n`app/http.py` and `fetch_status` are unchanged, and the e"
